{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs. realized prices, especially beyond ~10 trading days. Apply a downward level shift (small in days 1–5, larger from days 10–30).",
    "Downtrend underestimation (lag): when history shows a clear declining regime, the model follows the slope too slowly and predicts stabilization/rebound too early. Increase negative drift in the forecast if the last 2–4 weeks trend is down.",
    "Rebound/mean-reversion overshoot: the model repeatedly projects a bounce back toward mid-70s after dips, but realized prices continue falling or stay lower. Damp mean-reversion strength; cap rebounds unless confirmed by recent uptrend.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: forecast error magnitude increases with horizon (notably 15–30d), with widening overprediction. Use a horizon-scaled correction factor (progressively larger downward adjustment with lead time).",
    "Tail risk miss on selloffs: model fails to capture deeper drawdowns (realized lows in low-60s vs forecasts high-60s/70s). Add a downside extension rule when recent volatility + negative momentum are elevated (e.g., widen lower bound / apply extra downside skew)."
  ]
}
